
Bilkul 👍 Is topic ko DNS (Domain Name System) kehte hain. Isko simple Hinglish mein samjho.

🌐 DNS kya hai?

DNS ka full form hai:

Domain Name System

DNS ka main kaam hai domain name ko IP address mein translate karna.

Example:

tryhackme.com
      ↓
104.26.10.229


Humare liye tryhackme.com yaad rakhna easy hai, lekin computer/network communication ke liye IP address important hota hai.

🏠 Easy analogy

Socho tumhe kisi friend ke ghar jaana hai.

Tum friend ka naam jaante ho:

"Rahul"

Lekin delivery/postman ko actual address chahiye.

DNS bhi roughly wahi karta hai:

Domain Name = Naam
IP Address  = Address
DNS         = Naam → Address lookup service

🌳 Domain Hierarchy

Domain name ko right se left read karna useful hai.

Example:

www.tryhackme.com
│   │         │
│   │         └── TLD
│   └──────────── Second-Level Domain
└──────────────── Subdomain


Ab ek-ek karke:

1. TLD — Top-Level Domain

Domain ka sabse right wala part.

Example:

tryhackme.com
          ↑
         TLD


Yahan .com TLD hai.

Examples:

.com
.org
.edu
.gov
.uk
.in

gTLD

Generic Top-Level Domain:

.com
.org
.edu
.gov


Historically inka purpose indicate karna tha ki domain kis type ka hai.

ccTLD

Country Code TLD:

.in → India
.uk → United Kingdom
.ca → Canada

2. Second-Level Domain

Example:

tryhackme.com
^^^^^^^^^
   SLD


Yahan:

.com = TLD
tryhackme = Second-Level Domain

Ye basically domain ka main registered name hota hai.

3. Subdomain

Example:

admin.tryhackme.com
^^^^^
Subdomain


Yahan:

admin = Subdomain
tryhackme = Second-Level Domain
.com = TLD


Aur multiple subdomains bhi ho sakte hain:

jupiter.servers.tryhackme.com
^^^^^^^
Subdomain

🧠 Full breakdown
admin.tryhackme.com
  │       │       │
  │       │       └── TLD
  │       └────────── Second-Level Domain
  └────────────────── Subdomain

📋 DNS Record Types

DNS sirf domain → IPv4 nahi karta.

Different information ke liye different DNS records hote hain.

A Record

A = IPv4 address

example.com
    ↓
104.26.10.229


So:

A → IPv4

AAAA Record

AAAA = IPv6 address

Example:

example.com
     ↓
2606:4700:20::681a:be5


So:

AAAA → IPv6

Yaad rakhne ka trick:
A    → IPv4
AAAA → IPv6

🔗 CNAME Record

CNAME ka matlab hai:

Canonical Name

Ye directly IP address dene ke bajay another domain name par point karta hai.

Example:

store.tryhackme.com
        ↓
shops.shopify.com
        ↓
      IP Address


Matlab DNS ko ek extra lookup karna pad sakta hai.

Shortcut:

CNAME → Domain ka alias

📧 MX Record

MX = Mail Exchange

Ye batata hai ki domain ki email handle karne wali mail server kaunsi hai.

Example:

tryhackme.com
      ↓
MX
      ↓
mail server


MX records ke saath priority bhi hoti hai.

Example:

Priority 10 → Mail Server A
Priority 20 → Mail Server B


Generally lower priority number ko preference milti hai.

Agar primary mail server unavailable ho, backup server use ho sakta hai.

Shortcut:

MX → Email server

📝 TXT Record

TXT record basically domain ke saath text-based information store karne ke liye hota hai.

Iske multiple uses hain.

For example:

Email security

SPF/DKIM/DMARC related information publish karne ke liye use ho sakta hai.

Domain verification

Kisi third-party service ko prove karna ki:

"Haan, ye domain mere control mein hai."

Example:

example.com
TXT
"verification-token"

Shortcut:

TXT → Extra text/information

🚀 DNS Request ka actual process

Ab sabse important part.

Suppose tum browser mein type karte ho:

www.tryhackme.com


Computer ko IP chahiye.

Process roughly:

Your Computer
      ↓
Local DNS Cache
      ↓
Recursive DNS Server
      ↓
Root DNS Server
      ↓
.com TLD Server
      ↓
Authoritative DNS Server
      ↓
IP Address


Let's break it down.

1️⃣ Local Cache

Sabse pehle tumhara computer check karta hai:

"Kya mujhe is domain ka IP already pata hai?"

Agar cache mein answer mil gaya:

tryhackme.com
     ↓
104.26.10.229


Toh request yahin finish.

No need for further DNS lookup.

2️⃣ Recursive DNS Server

Agar local cache mein answer nahi hai, request Recursive DNS Server ko jaati hai.

Usually ye ISP provide karta hai, but tum different DNS resolver bhi configure kar sakte ho.

Recursive server bhi apna cache maintain karta hai.

Agar uske paas answer hai:

Recursive DNS
     ↓
IP Address


Done.

Agar nahi hai, toh woh DNS hierarchy ko query karega.

3️⃣ Root DNS Server 🌳

Agar recursive server ko answer nahi mila, woh Root DNS Server se poochta hai.

Root server ka role basically:

"Tum .com domain ke baare mein pooch rahe ho? Toh .com TLD server ke paas jao."

Example:

www.tryhackme.com
       ↓
Root
       ↓
.com TLD Server


Root server usually final IP answer nahi deta.

4️⃣ TLD Server

Ab request .com TLD server ke paas jaati hai.

TLD server kehta hai roughly:

"tryhackme.com ka authoritative nameserver ye hai."

For example:

.com TLD
    ↓
Authoritative DNS Server

5️⃣ Authoritative DNS Server

Ye sabse important DNS server hai for that domain.

Authoritative server ke paas actual DNS records stored hote hain.

Example:

tryhackme.com
      ↓
A Record
      ↓
104.26.10.229


Ye answer Recursive DNS Server ko return karta hai.

6️⃣ Answer tumhare computer tak

Final flow:

Authoritative DNS
       ↓
Recursive DNS
       ↓
Your Computer
       ↓
104.26.10.229
       ↓
Website connection


Phir browser IP address use karke website se connect karta hai.

⏱️ TTL — Time To Live

DNS records ke saath TTL value hoti hai.

TTL batati hai:

"Is DNS answer ko kitni der cache mein rakhna hai?"

Example:

TTL = 3600 seconds


Matlab roughly:

3600 sec = 1 hour


Cache 1 hour tak answer reuse kar sakta hai, depending on DNS behaviour.

Caching kyun useful hai?

Agar har baar complete DNS journey karni pade:

Computer
 ↓
Recursive
 ↓
Root
 ↓
TLD
 ↓
Authoritative


toh unnecessary traffic aur delay hoga.

Caching se:

Computer
 ↓
Cache
 ↓
IP


kaafi fast ho sakta hai.

🧠 Ek complete example

Tum type karte ho:

www.example.com

Step 1

Computer cache check:

❌ Not found

Step 2

Recursive DNS:

❌ Not cached

Step 3

Root:

.com → TLD server

Step 4

TLD:

example.com → Authoritative DNS

Step 5

Authoritative server:

A record
↓
93.x.x.x

Step 6

Answer wapas:

Authoritative
     ↓
Recursive
     ↓
Computer
     ↓
Browser

